ooo("V03-E-002", "Language and media",
    "Dubbing and subtitling each trade one kind of loss for another.", "Easy",
    ["Countries that import much of their television tend to settle on one of two habits: dubbing foreign programmes into the local language or subtitling them.",
     "Dubbing lets viewers watch without reading, but it replaces the actors' voices and often bends the dialogue to match the movement of their lips.",
     "Subtitling keeps the original performance intact, at the cost of asking the audience to divide its attention between the picture and the text.",
     "Neither choice is simply better, since each gives up one valuable thing in order to keep another."],
    "Television arrived in different countries at very different times, and in some it remained a state monopoly for decades.",
    "Sentence [[1]] names the two habits, sentences [[2]] and [[3]] weigh the gain and cost of each in turn, and sentence [[4]] concludes that each trades one loss for another. Sentence [[X]] mentions television but turns to when it arrived and who controlled it, which has nothing to do with the comparison of dubbing and subtitling.",
    "Treating any sentence that mentions television as part of a paragraph about translating programmes.",
    "V03E-subtitles-versus-dubbing",
    "The paragraph has a clear two-sided structure, and the odd sentence moves to broadcasting history, which is easy to spot.",
    traps=[(1, "Sentence [[1]] may seem like mere background, but it introduces the 'two habits' that sentences [[2]] and [[3]] then examine one by one.")],
    cognitive="Understanding")

ooo("V03-E-003", "Health and medicine",
    "Body mass index is a cheap population screen that cannot judge an individual's health.", "Easy",
    ["Body mass index, calculated from a person's weight and height, is popular because it is cheap, quick and needs no special equipment.",
     "Its simplicity, however, means that it cannot tell muscle from fat, so a heavily muscled athlete may be classed as overweight.",
     "Nor does it reveal where fat is stored, although fat around the abdomen is generally thought to carry greater risk than fat elsewhere.",
     "The index is therefore best treated as a rough screening tool for populations rather than as a verdict on any one person's health."],
    "Regular physical activity, even brisk walking for half an hour a day, is widely recommended for adults of every weight.",
    "Sentence [[1]] explains why the index is popular, sentences [[2]] and [[3]] give two limitations that follow from its simplicity ('Nor' links them), and sentence [[4]] concludes how it should be used. Sentence [[X]] is health advice about exercise; it does not concern how body mass index measures anything, so it breaks the evaluation of the index.",
    "Keeping a sentence because it sounds like sensible health advice, even though the paragraph is about the limits of a measurement.",
    "V03E-body-mass-index-limits",
    "The four sentences form an obvious praise, limits, verdict sequence, and the odd sentence turns to exercise.",
    traps=[(3, "Sentence [[3]] adds a new point about fat around the abdomen, but its opening 'Nor' marks it as the second limitation, continuing sentence [[2]].")],
    cognitive="Understanding")

ooo("V03-E-004", "Health and medicine",
    "How herd immunity works and why its threshold depends on contagiousness.", "Easy",
    ["When enough people in a community are immune to an infection, the disease struggles to find new hosts and outbreaks tend to fade out.",
     "This protection extends to those who cannot be vaccinated, such as newborn babies or patients whose immune systems are weakened.",
     "The share of the population that must be immune depends on how contagious the disease is; the more easily it spreads, the higher the threshold.",
     "For this reason, a small fall in vaccination rates can matter far more for a highly contagious disease than for a mildly contagious one."],
    "The first vaccines were developed long before scientists understood what viruses actually were.",
    "Sentence [[1]] describes the community effect, sentence [[2]] says 'this protection' covers those who cannot be vaccinated, sentence [[3]] explains that the required share depends on contagiousness, and sentence [[4]] draws the consequence 'for this reason'. Sentence [[X]] mentions vaccines but is about the history of their discovery, which does not connect to how community immunity works.",
    "Keeping a sentence because it uses the word 'vaccines', without checking that it contributes to the explanation of community protection.",
    "V03E-herd-immunity",
    "The links 'This protection' and 'For this reason' tie the four sentences together, and the odd sentence jumps to the past.",
    traps=[(2, "Sentence [[2]] can look like an aside about particular patients, but 'This protection' refers back to sentence [[1]] and shows why the community effect matters.")],
    cognitive="Understanding")

ooo("V03-E-005", "Psychology",
    "Mere familiarity makes things more liked, and that liking can pass for merit.", "Easy",
    ["People tend to grow fonder of things simply because they have encountered them before, even when they cannot recall the earlier encounters.",
     "A melody heard several times in the background, for instance, is often rated as more pleasant than one heard only once.",
     "Advertisers make use of this tendency when they repeat a brand name or a jingle far more often than any new information would justify.",
     "In such cases familiarity is quietly mistaken for genuine merit."],
    "Memory for faces is generally better than memory for names, which is why people often recognise someone they cannot name.",
    "Sentence [[1]] states the tendency to like what is familiar, sentence [[2]] gives an example, sentence [[3]] shows advertisers exploiting 'this tendency', and sentence [[4]] names the error involved. Sentence [[X]] is about memory, as sentence [[1]] partly is, but it compares remembering faces and names and says nothing about liking, so it does not belong.",
    "Linking sentence [[X]] to sentence [[1]] because both mention recall, without noticing that the paragraph is about preference, not memory.",
    "V03E-mere-exposure-effect",
    "The odd sentence borrows the idea of memory, but the paragraph's focus on liking is plain.",
    traps=[(3, "Sentence [[3]] moves to advertising, but it applies the tendency from sentence [[1]] and prepares the conclusion in sentence [[4]].")],
    cognitive="Understanding")

ooo("V03-E-006", "Education",
    "Worked examples help beginners because they remove the search for a method, but the benefit fades with skill.", "Easy",
    ["Beginners in mathematics often learn more from studying fully worked examples than from attempting problems on their own straight away.",
     "When a novice tries to solve a problem unaided, much of their attention goes into searching for a method, leaving little for understanding why it works.",
     "A worked example removes that search and lets the learner concentrate on the structure of the solution.",
     "As skill grows, however, the benefit shrinks, and practice without a model eventually becomes the better use of time."],
    "Anxiety about mathematics, a feeling of tension when faced with numbers, troubles many students and some adults throughout their lives.",
    "Sentence [[1]] makes the claim, sentence [[2]] explains the difficulty of unaided problem solving, sentence [[3]] shows how a worked example removes 'that search', and sentence [[4]] qualifies the claim for more skilled learners. Sentence [[X]] concerns mathematics learners but is about anxiety, which the argument about attention and worked examples never touches.",
    "Keeping sentence [[X]] because it is about students and mathematics, the same broad subject as the paragraph.",
    "V03E-worked-examples-in-maths",
    "The argument is a simple claim, reason and qualification, and the odd sentence introduces an unrelated emotional factor.",
    traps=[(4, "Sentence [[4]] seems to contradict sentence [[1]], but its 'however' marks a qualification: the advantage belongs to beginners, which is what sentence [[1]] said.")],
    cognitive="Understanding")

ooo("V03-E-007", "Health and medicine",
    "Emergency triage orders patients by urgency rather than arrival.", "Easy",
    ["In a busy emergency department, patients are not seen in the order in which they arrive but in the order of how urgently they need care.",
     "A nurse usually makes a quick assessment of each newcomer, checking breathing, pulse, pain and other warning signs.",
     "On that basis the patient is placed in a priority category, so that someone with chest pain may be seen before a person who has waited longer with a sprained wrist.",
     "The system can feel unfair to those kept waiting, but it is designed so that delay falls on those who can best afford it."],
    "Ambulance crews are trained to keep patients stable during the journey and to pass on key details when they arrive at the hospital.",
    "Sentence [[1]] states the principle of seeing patients by urgency, sentence [[2]] describes the assessment, sentence [[3]] ('On that basis') shows the resulting priority, and sentence [[4]] answers the charge of unfairness. Sentence [[X]] is about emergency care but concerns what happens before arrival; it plays no part in how the department decides the order of treatment.",
    "Accepting sentence [[X]] because ambulances belong to emergency medicine, without asking whether it explains the ordering of patients.",
    "V03E-emergency-triage",
    "The paragraph is a clear step-by-step process, and the odd sentence describes a different stage of care.",
    traps=[(4, "Sentence [[4]] shifts to fairness, but it defends the system described in sentences [[1]], [[2]] and [[3]] against the obvious complaint of those kept waiting.")],
    cognitive="Understanding")

ooo("V03-E-008", "Language and media",
    "Punctuation began as a guide for reading aloud and became a guide for the eye.", "Easy",
    ["Many early writing systems ran words together without spaces or punctuation, leaving readers to work out the divisions for themselves.",
     "Such texts were usually read aloud, and the reader supplied the pauses and emphasis from knowledge of the language.",
     "As silent, private reading spread, marks that showed the structure of a sentence on the page became more and more useful.",
     "Punctuation thus began as a guide to the voice and gradually became a guide to the eye."],
    "Spelling in many languages has changed more slowly than pronunciation, so that written words often preserve sounds that are no longer spoken.",
    "Sentence [[1]] describes unpunctuated early texts, sentence [[2]] explains how 'such texts' were read aloud, sentence [[3]] shows why marks became useful once reading turned silent, and sentence [[4]] sums up the shift from voice to eye. Sentence [[X]] is about writing and speech too, but it concerns spelling and sound change, not the purpose of punctuation.",
    "Keeping sentence [[X]] because it relates writing to speech, the same pairing the paragraph uses, without noticing that it is about spelling.",
    "V03E-punctuation-and-speech-rhythm",
    "The paragraph is chronological with a summing-up sentence, and the odd sentence is about a different feature of writing.",
    traps=[(2, "Sentence [[2]] may look like a side remark on reading aloud, but it supplies the 'voice' half of the conclusion in sentence [[4]].")],
    cognitive="Understanding")

