ooo("V03-E-009", "Language and media",
    "Sign languages are independent languages with their own grammar, not coded speech.", "Medium",
    ["It is often assumed that sign languages are simply spoken languages translated into gestures, word for word.",
     "In fact, established sign languages have their own grammar, using space, movement and facial expression to mark relations that speech marks with word order or endings.",
     "This independence is why two countries that share a spoken language can use sign languages that the signers of each find largely unintelligible.",
     "Recognising sign languages as full languages rather than as codes has consequences for the way deaf children are taught."],
    "Facial expression also carries emotion in spoken conversation, and listeners rely on it more than they usually realise.",
    "Sentence [[1]] states the common assumption, sentence [[2]] rejects it ('In fact') by describing an independent grammar, sentence [[3]] gives the evidence of 'this independence', and sentence [[4]] draws a consequence. Sentence [[X]] picks up the phrase 'facial expression' from sentence [[2]], but it is about emotion in spoken conversation, which does not bear on whether sign languages are full languages.",
    "Attaching sentence [[X]] to sentence [[2]] because both mention facial expression, though one is about grammar and the other about emotion in speech.",
    "V03E-sign-languages-as-full-languages",
    "The odd sentence repeats a central phrase from the paragraph, so it must be rejected because its function differs, not because its vocabulary does.",
    traps=[(4, "Sentence [[4]] turns to teaching, but it is the practical consequence of the argument that sign languages are 'full languages rather than as codes'."),
           (1, "Sentence [[1]] states a view the paragraph rejects, but without it the 'In fact' of sentence [[2]] has nothing to answer.")])

ooo("V03-E-010", "Language and media",
    "Dictionaries record usage, so their authority is borrowed from speakers.", "Medium",
    ["Many readers treat a dictionary as a rulebook that tells them how words ought to be used.",
     "Most modern lexicographers, however, regard their job as describing the language people really speak and write, which they do by gathering and studying vast numbers of real examples.",
     "When a new sense of a word becomes common enough, it enters the dictionary, whatever purists may think of it.",
     "The authority that readers grant the dictionary is therefore, in a sense, borrowed from the speakers whose habits it records."],
    "Older dictionaries were often compiled by a single scholar over many years, whereas modern ones are usually produced by large teams.",
    "Sentence [[1]] gives the rulebook view, sentence [[2]] contrasts it with the lexicographers' aim of recording use, sentence [[3]] shows that aim in action, and sentence [[4]] concludes that the dictionary's authority comes from speakers. Sentence [[X]] contrasts old and modern dictionaries, but on who compiles them, not on whether they prescribe or record, so it does not advance the argument.",
    "Linking sentence [[X]] to sentence [[2]] because both speak of modern dictionary makers, though the paragraph's contrast is between prescribing and recording.",
    "V03E-dictionaries-describing-usage",
    "The odd sentence offers an old-versus-modern contrast that resembles the paragraph's contrast, but on a different dimension.",
    traps=[(1, "Sentence [[1]] states a view the paragraph corrects, but sentence [[2]]'s 'however' and sentence [[4]]'s 'authority that readers grant' both depend on it.")])

ooo("V03-E-011", "Education",
    "Mother-tongue instruction is a claim about the order in which languages are learned.", "Medium",
    ["Children who begin school in a language they do not speak at home face two tasks at once: learning the language and learning through it.",
     "When early lessons are given in the mother tongue, the second task can begin at once, because the child already understands what the teacher says.",
     "Reading skills built in a familiar language also tend to carry over, so that learning to read a second language later becomes easier.",
     "The case for mother-tongue instruction is thus not a rejection of other languages but a claim about the order in which they are best learned."],
    "Children who grow up hearing two languages at home sometimes mix words from both in a single sentence, a habit that usually fades with age.",
    "Sentence [[1]] sets out the two tasks, sentence [[2]] shows how the mother tongue removes the first, sentence [[3]] adds that early reading skills carry over to a second language, and sentence [[4]] concludes that the case concerns order, not rejection. Sentence [[X]] is about children and two languages, but it describes mixing languages at home, which says nothing about the language of schooling.",
    "Keeping sentence [[X]] because it is about bilingual children, without seeing that the paragraph concerns the language of instruction.",
    "V03E-mother-tongue-early-schooling",
    "The odd sentence shares the subject of children and two languages and must be rejected on its focus.",
    traps=[(3, "Sentence [[3]] introduces a second language, which can make it look out of place, but it is what allows sentence [[4]] to say the case is about order rather than rejection.")])

ooo("V03-E-012", "Education",
    "Ability grouping helps or harms depending on how lower groups are taught.", "Medium",
    ["Sorting pupils into classes by ability is often defended on the ground that teachers can pitch lessons more precisely to a narrower range of learners.",
     "The evidence is more mixed than this argument suggests, because gains for the top groups are frequently offset by losses for those placed lower down.",
     "Pupils in lower groups may be given less demanding work and come to see themselves as weak, which can lower their effort further.",
     "Whether grouping helps, then, depends less on the sorting itself than on whether lower groups are taught with the same ambition as the rest."],
    "Some schools group pupils by age, while others run mixed-age classes in which older children help the younger ones.",
    "Sentence [[1]] gives the case for ability grouping, sentence [[2]] says the evidence is mixed because of losses lower down, sentence [[3]] explains those losses, and sentence [[4]] concludes what the outcome depends on. Sentence [[X]] also concerns grouping pupils, but by age, a different question that the argument about ability classes never takes up.",
    "Treating sentence [[X]] as relevant because it uses the word 'group', though it changes the basis of grouping from ability to age.",
    "V03E-ability-grouping-in-classrooms",
    "The odd sentence shares the word 'group' and the school setting, so it must be rejected on function.",
    traps=[(3, "Sentence [[3]] may seem like a separate point about self-image, but it explains the 'losses for those placed lower down' in sentence [[2]].")])

ooo("V03-E-013", "Education",
    "Open-book examinations remove the reward for memorising, not the need to prepare.", "Medium",
    ["Many people think an open-book paper must be a soft option, since a candidate with the textbook at hand can presumably just copy out what is needed.",
     "In practice, a well-designed open-book paper asks questions whose answers cannot be found on any page, such as applying a principle to an unfamiliar case.",
     "Students who have not understood the material soon discover that the book is of little help under time pressure.",
     "What the format removes is the reward for memorising, not the need for preparation."],
    "Examinations taken at home without supervision raise obvious questions about whether the work submitted is really the student's own.",
    "Sentence [[1]] states the belief that open-book papers are a soft option, sentence [[2]] answers it by describing questions no page answers, sentence [[3]] shows what happens to unprepared students, and sentence [[4]] concludes what the format does and does not remove. Sentence [[X]] is about a kind of examination, but it raises the problem of honesty in unsupervised work, which is not the point about whether open-book papers are easy.",
    "Assuming sentence [[X]] belongs because unsupervised and open-book examinations both relax ordinary rules, though they raise different questions.",
    "V03E-open-book-examinations",
    "The odd sentence sounds like another objection to the format, so a solver must see that it targets a different kind of test and a different concern.",
    traps=[(3, "Sentence [[3]] may look like a passing remark, but it gives the practical proof that sentence [[4]]'s 'need for preparation' remains.")])

ooo("V03-E-014", "Health and medicine",
    "Fever is part of the body's defence, so reducing every fever may not aid recovery.", "Medium",
    ["Fever is usually treated as something to be brought down, yet a raised temperature is part of the body's response to infection rather than merely a symptom of it.",
     "Many microbes multiply less efficiently at higher temperatures, and some immune cells appear to work more effectively when the body is warmer.",
     "This has led some physicians to question the habit of reducing every moderate fever with medication.",
     "They do not deny that very high fevers can be dangerous; their point is that comfort and recovery are not always served by the same treatment."],
    "A thermometer placed under the tongue usually gives a slightly different reading from one placed in the ear or under the arm.",
    "Sentence [[1]] claims that fever is part of the defence, sentence [[2]] gives the reasons, sentence [[3]] ('This') reports the doubt about routine treatment that follows, and sentence [[4]] states its limits. Sentence [[X]] is about body temperature, but it concerns how temperature is measured, which has no bearing on whether a fever helps the body fight infection.",
    "Keeping sentence [[X]] because it is about temperature, the paragraph's central term, though it concerns measurement rather than the role of fever.",
    "V03E-fever-as-defence",
    "The odd sentence shares the paragraph's central term, and the closing caution about very high fevers can look detachable.",
    traps=[(4, "Sentence [[4]] begins with 'They', referring to the physicians of sentence [[3]], and qualifies their view; it is needed so that the paragraph does not overstate the case.")])

ooo("V03-E-015", "Cities and geography",
    "Delta subsidence is largely human-made and therefore within reach of local policy.", "Medium",
    ["Many of the world's great river deltas are sinking, and in some places the land is falling faster than the sea is rising.",
     "Part of the cause is the pumping of groundwater, which allows the soft sediments beneath delta cities to compact.",
     "Another is the dams built upstream, which trap the sediment that rivers once carried down to rebuild the delta in each flood season.",
     "Because these causes are largely of human making, they are, unlike the rising sea, within reach of local policy."],
    "Sea levels rise unevenly across the globe, because ocean currents, winds and the pull of the great ice sheets differ from one region to another.",
    "Sentence [[1]] states that deltas are sinking, sentences [[2]] and [[3]] give two causes ('Part of the cause', 'Another'), and sentence [[4]] concludes that these human causes can be addressed locally. Sentence [[X]] shares the topic of rising seas, but it explains why sea level varies by region, whereas the paragraph is about the land sinking for human reasons.",
    "Attaching sentence [[X]] to sentence [[1]] because both compare land and sea, without noticing that the argument is about the causes of sinking land.",
    "V03E-sinking-river-deltas",
    "The odd sentence uses the paragraph's language of rising seas and looks like useful context, so it must be rejected on function.",
    traps=[(3, "Sentence [[3]] moves upstream to dams, but 'Another' marks it as the second cause that sentence [[4]] relies on.")])

ooo("V03-E-016", "Cities and geography",
    "Congestion charges ration scarce road space by price; fairness then turns on use of revenue.", "Medium",
    ["Charging drivers to enter a crowded city centre at busy hours is often opposed as a tax on people who have no choice but to drive.",
     "Its advocates reply that road space at peak times is a scarce good, and that giving it away free simply rations it by queuing instead of by price.",
     "A modest charge persuades some drivers to travel earlier, share a car or take a bus, and the traffic that remains moves faster.",
     "The fairness objection is not thereby answered, but it shifts to the question of how the money raised is spent."],
    "Many cities have also introduced low-emission zones, which charge or ban the most polluting vehicles regardless of the time of day.",
    "Sentence [[1]] states the objection, sentence [[2]] gives the advocates' reply about scarce road space, sentence [[3]] describes the effect of a charge, and sentence [[4]] returns to the objection and relocates it. Sentence [[X]] mentions charging vehicles, but low-emission zones aim at pollution and apply 'regardless of the time of day', so they do not belong to an argument about rationing road space at busy hours.",
    "Taking sentence [[X]] as another example of the same policy because it involves charging drivers, without noting its different purpose.",
    "V03E-congestion-pricing",
    "The odd sentence uses the paragraph's vocabulary of charges and cities, so the solver must see that its goal is different.",
    traps=[(4, "Sentence [[4]] may seem to undercut the advocates, but it completes the exchange begun in sentence [[1]] by showing where the fairness objection now lies.")])

ooo("V03-E-017", "Psychology",
    "Crowds can reduce help because responsibility spreads and onlookers read calm as safety.", "Medium",
    ["A person in distress in a crowded place is not necessarily more likely to receive help than one who collapses on a quiet street.",
     "Each onlooker may assume that someone else has already acted, or is better placed to act, and the responsibility spreads so thin that no one takes it up.",
     "Onlookers also watch one another for cues, and when everyone appears calm, each concludes that the situation must not be an emergency.",
     "Singling out one person and asking them directly for help can therefore break the deadlock, because it hands the responsibility back to an individual."],
    "People are generally more willing to help someone who resembles them, whether in age, dress or manner of speaking.",
    "Sentence [[1]] states the puzzle, sentences [[2]] and [[3]] give two reasons a crowd can fail to act ('also' links them), and sentence [[4]] draws the practical remedy from the first reason. Sentence [[X]] is about helping, but it concerns similarity between helper and victim, not the effect of the number of onlookers, so it does not fit the argument.",
    "Keeping sentence [[X]] because it explains when people help, though the paragraph is specifically about crowds diffusing responsibility.",
    "V03E-bystander-effect",
    "The odd sentence offers another plausible factor in helping, so it must be rejected because it does not concern crowds.",
    traps=[(3, "Sentence [[3]] adds a second mechanism, which can make it look like a digression, but its 'also' shows it is a further reason for the puzzle in sentence [[1]].")])

ooo("V03-E-018", "Psychology",
    "Hindsight makes outcomes seem predictable, so decisions must be judged by what was knowable then.", "Medium",
    ["Once we know how events turned out, we tend to believe that we could have predicted the outcome all along.",
     "Information that pointed towards the actual result seems, in retrospect, to have been obvious, while the signals that pointed elsewhere fade from memory.",
     "This distortion makes us harsh judges of decision-makers, who are blamed for failing to see what became clear only afterwards.",
     "A fair assessment of a decision must therefore ask what was knowable at the time, not what is known now."],
    "Experts in some fields make forecasts that are barely more accurate than chance, yet they remain confident in them.",
    "Sentence [[1]] names the tendency, sentence [[2]] explains how it works, sentence [[3]] shows its effect on how we judge others ('This distortion'), and sentence [[4]] states the remedy. Sentence [[X]] is about prediction, but it concerns the accuracy of forecasts made before events, whereas the paragraph is about how knowing an outcome distorts judgement afterwards.",
    "Accepting sentence [[X]] because it also speaks of prediction and confidence, without noticing that it looks forward while the paragraph looks back.",
    "V03E-hindsight-bias",
    "The odd sentence uses the paragraph's language of prediction and confidence, so the solver must separate forecasting from hindsight.",
    traps=[(3, "Sentence [[3]] moves from memory to blame, but it supplies the reason for the rule about fair assessment in sentence [[4]].")])

ooo("V03-E-019", "Psychology",
    "People underestimate their own project times; the outside view of comparable cases corrects this.", "Medium",
    ["People routinely underestimate how long their own projects will take, even when they know that similar projects in the past ran late.",
     "The error arises partly because planners imagine the specific steps of the task and picture them going smoothly, leaving no room for the delays that usually intrude.",
     "Interestingly, people are often more realistic about other people's projects, which they see from the outside, without the vivid detail of each step.",
     "This suggests a remedy: before planning the details, ask how long comparable projects have actually taken, and adjust from there."],
    "Deadlines set by others often lead people to work harder in the final days before them than at any earlier point.",
    "Sentence [[1]] states the error, sentence [[2]] explains it by the focus on specific steps, sentence [[3]] notes that outsiders without that detail judge better, and sentence [[4]] turns this into a remedy. Sentence [[X]] concerns deadlines and effort, but it is about when people work hard, not about why they misjudge how long a task will take.",
    "Keeping sentence [[X]] because deadlines seem naturally linked to projects running late, though it describes effort, not estimation.",
    "V03E-planning-fallacy",
    "The odd sentence sits in the same setting of projects and deadlines, so it must be rejected on its different point.",
    traps=[(3, "Sentence [[3]] shifts to other people's projects, but it is the observation that 'This suggests a remedy' in sentence [[4]] builds on.")])

