ooo("V03-E-020", "Philosophy and ethics",
    "The ship puzzle shows that sameness rests on two criteria, and choosing between them depends on purpose.", "Difficult",
    ["Suppose a ship has its timbers renewed piece by piece over many years until none of the original wood remains; it seems natural to say that the same ship is still sailing.",
     "Yet if someone gathered the discarded planks and reassembled them, that reconstructed vessel would also have a strong claim to be the original.",
     "The puzzle suggests that our idea of sameness combines two criteria, continuity of form and history and continuity of material, which normally coincide and come apart only in unusual cases.",
     "Asking which ship is 'really' the original may then be less a question about the world than about which criterion we have reason to favour for a given purpose."],
    "This shows that the material of an object, rather than its history, is what finally fixes its identity.",
    "Sentences [[1]] and [[2]] set up two rival claims, sentence [[3]] explains the conflict as two criteria that normally coincide, and sentence [[4]] concludes that choosing between them depends on purpose. Sentence [[X]] uses the same vocabulary but declares material the winner, which contradicts the paragraph: sentence [[1]] treats the replaced ship as plausibly the same, and sentences [[3]] and [[4]] deny that one criterion simply settles the matter.",
    "Reading sentence [[X]] as a natural conclusion to the reassembly case in sentence [[2]], without checking it against the paragraph's actual conclusion.",
    "V03E-ship-of-theseus-identity",
    "The odd sentence is on the same puzzle and sounds like a conclusion; it is rejected only because it asserts a resolution the paragraph's argument refuses.",
    traps=[(2, "Sentence [[2]] seems to undercut sentence [[1]], but the paragraph needs both rival claims, since sentence [[3]] calls their conflict 'the puzzle'."),
           (4, "Sentence [[4]] may look like a vague afterthought, but it is the conclusion that sentence [[3]]'s two criteria lead to.")],
    exams=DX)

ooo("V03-E-021", "Philosophy and ethics",
    "Moral consideration turns on the capacity to suffer, not on intelligence.", "Difficult",
    ["The question of whether animals matter morally is often framed as a question about intelligence, as though cleverer creatures deserved more consideration.",
     "But the capacity that seems most relevant to how a being may be treated is not the ability to reason but the ability to suffer.",
     "A creature that can feel pain has an interest in not being hurt, whether or not it can solve puzzles or use tools.",
     "On this view, the hard questions concern which animals can suffer and how much, and these are questions about evidence that tests of intelligence cannot settle."],
    "What deserves protection, some argue, is not the individual animal but the species, whose loss is final in a way that a single death is not.",
    "Sentence [[1]] gives the common framing, sentence [[2]] replaces intelligence with the ability to suffer, sentence [[3]] explains why suffering grounds an interest, and sentence [[4]] says what the hard questions then become. Sentence [[X]] also discusses what deserves moral protection, but it moves from the individual creature to the species, a change of subject that the argument about each being's capacity to suffer never addresses.",
    "Taking sentence [[X]] as another rival to the intelligence view, without seeing that it changes the bearer of moral concern from individuals to species.",
    "V03E-animal-pain-moral-status",
    "The odd sentence offers a serious alternative view in the same moral vocabulary, so it must be rejected because it answers a different question.",
    traps=[(1, "Sentence [[1]] states a view the paragraph rejects, but sentence [[2]]'s 'But' and sentence [[4]]'s reference to tests of intelligence both depend on it.")],
    exams=DX)

ooo("V03-E-022", "Philosophy and ethics",
    "Foreseeable harm, not the present existence of victims, grounds duties to future people.", "Difficult",
    ["A familiar argument holds that duties to the unborn are impossible, because a duty must be owed to someone and the unborn are, as yet, no one at all.",
     "The argument proves too much, however: someone who buried a device set to explode in a hundred years would plainly be doing wrong, though every eventual victim is yet unborn.",
     "What makes the act wrong is not the present existence of its victims but the foreseeable harm it will cause to whoever comes to exist.",
     "If that is right, the absence of present persons is no bar to obligations concerning pollution, debt or the depletion of resources whose effects arrive long after us."],
    "Some economists hold that benefits in the future should count for less than benefits now, simply because they lie further ahead in time.",
    "Sentence [[1]] states the argument that we owe nothing to future people, sentence [[2]] refutes it with the buried device, sentence [[3]] identifies what makes that act wrong, and sentence [[4]] extends the principle to real policy. Sentence [[X]] is about the future, but it asks how much future benefits should weigh, not whether obligations to people not yet born can exist at all, so it does not belong.",
    "Linking sentence [[X]] to sentence [[4]] because both concern policies with distant effects, though discounting is a different question from whether future people can be owed anything.",
    "V03E-duties-to-future-generations",
    "The odd sentence comes from the same debate about the future and could pass as an objection, so the solver must see that the paragraph's question is existence, not weight.",
    traps=[(2, "Sentence [[2]] reads like a vivid digression, but 'proves too much' marks it as the refutation that sentence [[3]] then explains.")],
    exams=DX)

ooo("V03-E-023", "Philosophy and ethics",
    "Ordinary morality allows acts beyond duty, which some theories struggle to accommodate.", "Difficult",
    ["Ordinary morality distinguishes between what we are required to do and what it would be admirable, but not obligatory, to do.",
     "A passer-by who pulls a drowning child from shallow water does only what is required, whereas one who dives into a freezing river at great risk does more than duty demands.",
     "Some moral theories have trouble with this distinction, because if one should always do the best act available, no act can go beyond what is required.",
     "Defenders of the ordinary view reply that a morality leaving no room for heroism beyond duty would ask too much of people and leave too little to their own choice."],
    "Heroic acts are often performed on impulse, and those who perform them commonly insist afterwards that anyone would have done the same.",
    "Sentence [[1]] states the distinction, sentence [[2]] illustrates it, sentence [[3]] shows why some theories cannot accommodate it, and sentence [[4]] gives the defenders' reply. Sentence [[X]] is about heroic acts, but it describes how heroes act and speak, a psychological observation that bears neither on whether heroism goes beyond duty nor on the theoretical dispute.",
    "Reading sentence [[X]] as support for the view that heroism is merely required, because heroes say 'anyone would have done the same', though the paragraph's debate is about moral theory, not what heroes say.",
    "V03E-supererogation-beyond-duty",
    "The odd sentence is on heroism and even seems to speak to the dispute, so a solver must see that a hero's modesty settles nothing about duty.",
    traps=[(2, "Sentence [[2]] can look like a mere illustration that could be dropped, but it gives the concrete contrast that sentence [[3]]'s 'this distinction' and the idea of going beyond duty rely on.")],
    exams=DX)

ooo("V03-E-024", "Psychology",
    "Vivid memories of shocking news are marked by confidence, not accuracy.", "Difficult",
    ["Memories of where we were when we heard shocking news feel unusually vivid, and people hold them with great confidence.",
     "Yet when researchers have questioned people soon after such an event and again years later, the two accounts have often differed in important details.",
     "What sets these memories apart, it seems, is the certainty and feeling with which they are recalled rather than any special fidelity to what happened.",
     "Their vividness may owe less to the moment itself than to the many times the story has been retold and rehearsed since."],
    "Because such memories are shared by so many people, they offer historians a valuable record of how ordinary people lived through great events.",
    "Sentence [[1]] describes the vivid, confident memories, sentence [[2]] ('Yet') shows that accounts change over time, sentence [[3]] concludes that confidence rather than accuracy marks them, and sentence [[4]] offers an explanation of their vividness. Sentence [[X]] turns to the value of these memories for historians, a different focus, and its praise of them as a 'valuable record' sits awkwardly with the paragraph's point that they are unreliable.",
    "Accepting sentence [[X]] because it is about the same memories, without noticing that it shifts to their use as historical evidence and ignores their unreliability.",
    "V03E-flashbulb-memories",
    "The odd sentence follows naturally from the opening topic, and only the argument about accuracy shows that it cannot belong.",
    traps=[(4, "Sentence [[4]] may look like a new idea, but it explains where the vividness of sentence [[1]] comes from once sentence [[3]] has separated vividness from accuracy.")],
    exams=DX)

ooo("V03-E-025", "Cities and geography",
    "The grid's indifference to site is a design for uncertainty rather than a lack of design.", "Difficult",
    ["The regular street grid is often criticised as a monotonous imposition, the work of surveyors who drew straight lines without regard to the ground.",
     "Yet the grid's indifference to particular sites is also what made it useful to growing cities, since land could be divided, sold and built on before anyone knew what would stand there.",
     "The same blocks could hold houses, then shops, then offices, without the plan of the streets having to change.",
     "What critics read as a lack of design may thus be better understood as a design for uncertainty."],
    "Whether a street is pleasant to walk along depends more on the width of its pavements and the shade of its trees than on the geometry of the plan.",
    "Sentence [[1]] states the criticism, sentence [[2]] ('Yet') turns the grid's indifference into an advantage, sentence [[3]] shows the advantage over time, and sentence [[4]] reframes the criticism as a misreading. Sentence [[X]] is about streets and plans, but it sets geometry aside as unimportant to walking, whereas the paragraph argues that the grid's geometry serves a purpose, so it does not fit.",
    "Taking sentence [[X]] as another reply to the critics of sentence [[1]], though it dismisses the grid's geometry rather than defending it.",
    "V03E-street-grid-plans",
    "The odd sentence seems to answer the critics too, so the solver must see that it changes the question from adaptability to pleasantness.",
    traps=[(3, "Sentence [[3]] may seem a minor elaboration, but it supplies the change over time that makes the grid a 'design for uncertainty' in sentence [[4]].")],
    exams=DX)
